\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces \textsc {KGA}{} vs.\ trivial policies on the CIFAR-10-C Tent stress grid (432 conditions, ResNet-18, MPS, $\alpha {=}0.1$). Source: \texttt {decisive\_tta\_results.json}.}}{99}{table.8.1}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces CIFAR-10-C pre-registered stress grid (432 conditions per candidate, ResNet-18, MPS, $\alpha {=}0.1$). Source: \texttt {decisive\_tta\_results.json} and \texttt {decisive\_tta\_table.md}.}}{100}{table.8.2}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces CIFAR-10-C per-corruption suite: 13 corruptions $\times $ 5 severities ($=65$ cells), ResNet-18, $N{=}800$/cell. Adaptation is overwhelmingly helpful (false-adapt $0.015$); \textsc {KGA}{} matches always-adapt at equal safety. This is the helpful-dominated control. Source: \texttt {cifar10c\_suite\_results.json}.}}{103}{table.8.3}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.4}{\ignorespaces Online non-stationary CIFAR-10 TTA, 3 seeds per regime. Always-adapt collapses in the harm-dominated stream; always-freeze forgoes gains in the helpful stream; \textsc {KGA}{} is near-best in both and achieves the highest cross-regime mean. Harm-regime standard deviations: freeze $0.009$, adapt $0.025$, \textsc {KGA}{} $0.009$. Source: \texttt {cifar\_tent\_online\_results.json} (harsh stream) and \texttt {kbound.tex} Table~10.}}{104}{table.8.4}%
